  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Cash Transfers for Climate Resilience in Malawi</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Project aims to deliver direct and unconditional cash transfers to adult individuals in vulnerable, predominantly farming households in Chikwawa District. The cash transfers will be layered on top of a package of complementary interventions e.g. financial literacy training, entrepreneurship and business training, soil testing implementation of regenerative agricultural practices.GiveDirectly aims to directly reach up to 7,200 adult individuals (up to 3,200 in Year 1 and up to 4,000 in Year 2</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The impact of the project is:Increase in number of climate resilient individuals: a. Regular and sufficient household income.b. Increased capacity to adapt, anticipate and absorb climate extreme &amp; disasters, c. Reduced vulnerability to climate change, d. Enhanced well being of recipient households.The proposed response will address the following key gaps:Lack of resources to meet immediate needs: Due to the impacts of El Nino, which are causing shortfalls in agricultural production, combined with high food and non-food commodity prices, and limited livelihood options, smallholder farmers are lacking the resources to meet their basic needs and may resort to negative coping strategies which will hamper their livelihoods, resilience and wellbeing over the longer term.Limited access to capital and tools to build adaptive, absorptive and anticipatory resilience capacity: Due to the recurrent cycle of food insecurity and weather-related climate shocks, and the persistent macro-economic challenges in Malawi, smallholder farmers don&#x2019;t have the capacity to build their resilience and develop sustainable livelihoods.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Unconditional cash transfers to adult individuals in vulnerable, predominantly farming households in Chikwawa District in Southern Malawi.The cash transfers will be delivered in two separate transfers:1st Transfer: Token transfer of &#xA3;43.50 GBP2nd Transfer: Larger transfer of &#xA3;389.46 GBPThis transfer amount would enable recipients to meet basic consumption needs during the project while also providing them with sufficient capital to build their resilience ahead of the production and harvest seasons.</narrative>
  </description>
